@page "/docs/registry/authentication"
<PageTitle>Registry authentication - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Authentication</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">private registries</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">A registry behind a token is recorded like any other, with the headers or query parameters its requests have to carry. Credentials come from the environment: <code>blaizio.json</code> stores the variable's name, never its value.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Recording one</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/AuthAdd" />
        <p><code>--header</code> takes <code>name: value</code> and <code>--param</code> takes <code>name=value</code>; both repeat, and both apply to every registry recorded by that one command. Everything after the first separator is the value, so a header carrying colons survives intact.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">What lands in the config</h2>
        <p>A public registry stays a plain URL string. One with credentials becomes an object, and the entries beside it are left exactly as they were:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/AuthConfig" />
        <p>Headers go on the request; parameters are appended to the query string. Both are sent with every request to that registry: the catalogue read by <CmdChip Command="blaizio search" />, and each item fetched by <CmdChip Command="blaizio add" />, <CmdChip Command="blaizio view" /> or <CmdChip Command="blaizio update" />.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Where the secret lives</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>In the environment, always.</b> <code>${VAR}</code> is expanded when a request is made, from the environment of the shell that ran the command.</li>
            <li><b>Never in the file.</b> Recording a literal value works, and the CLI warns when you do it, because that value is about to be committed.</li>
            <li><b>Never in the output.</b> <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry list" /> prints the header and parameter NAMES, not their values, and a credential passed as a query parameter is kept out of error messages.</li>
            <li><b>Only when used.</b> Variables are read by the command that touches that registry, so recording one you have no token for today does not break unrelated commands tomorrow.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">What the errors mean</h2>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                    <BzTableHead>You see</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>It means</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">needs <code>${VAR}</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Local, and nothing was sent: the variable is not set in this shell. The message names the registry, the variable and which header wanted it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Not authorized</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>401. The credential was missing, wrong or expired. If the registry sent an explanation, it is quoted after the message.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Access denied</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>403. The credential was accepted, but it does not cover the item you asked for.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Rate limited</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>429. Wait and retry; the registry's own message says how long, when it sends one.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Serving one</h2>
        <p>There is nothing special to implement. A private registry is the same two-layer output as a public one, behind whatever check your host already does: read the header, decide, and answer <code>401</code> or <code>403</code> with a JSON body carrying a <code>message</code>. That message is shown to whoever ran the command, so spend a sentence on it: "Token expired, request a new one at ..." saves a support round trip.</p>
        <p>Serve over HTTPS. A header on a plain <code>http://</code> request is a token in the clear, and the CLI will not pretend otherwise.</p>
    </section>
</div>
